            <div xml:id="d44">

                <pb facs="0065" n="55" sameAs="frus:frus1943China/pg55"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/29511: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Atcheson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-05-22T15:00:00+07:00">May 22, 1943—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 5 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>760. Churchill’s address on May 19, before the Congress<anchor n="68"
                        xml:id="d44-f1"/> is characterized as “encouraging” by the Chinese press
                    which has, of course, given it considerable prominence and comment. However,
                    from remarks volunteered by Chinese officials in private conversation, it is
                    apparent that the speech has failed to dissipate the cynicism with which British
                    intentions and promises in regard to the question of the defeat of Japan seems
                    generally to be viewed in official Chinese circles here. Unfortunately it does
                    not appear likely that this attitude will alter materially until some concrete
                    British military success against the Japanese demonstrates that the defeat of
                    Japan at the earliest possible moment is a British objective for which, in due
                    course, they will strive whole-heartedly and with every possible resource.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Atcheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
